\title{Figures and Tables Style Guide}
\author{Ivan Jacob Agaloos Pesigan}
\date{}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

One source gives the paper's figure, the website's figure, and the slide's figure.
Talks have their own leeway (the talks guide).
Agents follow \texttt{.setup/ai/style/figures-tables.md},
which follows this guide and is changed together with it.

\section{Pipeline}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\hline
Kind & Source & Paper & Web \\
\hline
Result plot & \texttt{Fig<Name>()} in \texttt{R} & PDF & PNG \\
Diagram & \texttt{.setup/latex/tikz/<name>.tex} & PDF & PNG \\
Table & results in \texttt{.setup/data-*}, columns by name & \texttt{booktabs} & \texttt{knitr::kable()} \\
Animation & \texttt{Fig<Name>()}, one frame per call & small multiples & GIF \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{Plots}

\begin{itemize}
	\item Palette: Okabe-Ito (\texttt{grDevices::palette.colors()}),
	with a point shape and a line type per group, so that figures read in greyscale.
	\item Format: vector PDF for papers and slides,
	PNG at 300 dpi for pkgdown, Quarto, and the README; both from the same source.
	\item A \texttt{Fig<Name>()} is exported, documented, and tested.
	It saves the files itself, at a size preset, in \texttt{.setup/latex/figures}.
	\item A \texttt{highlight} or \texttt{subset} argument is optional,
	for a figure likely to be shown in stages:
	each stage is one call, shown with \texttt{\textbackslash only<n>\{\}} in Beamer.
\end{itemize}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
\texttt{size} & Width \(\times\) height (in) & Use \\
\hline
\texttt{"column"} & 3.3 \(\times\) 2.5 & one column of \texttt{jou}; body-text font \\
\texttt{"page"} & 6.5 \(\times\) 4 & text width; body-text font \\
\texttt{"slide"} & 5.5 \(\times\) 3.1 & 16:9 frame; larger sans-serif font \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The height can be overridden.
\LaTeX{} sets \texttt{width = \textbackslash linewidth} or \texttt{\textbackslash textwidth}, never \texttt{scale}.

\section{Diagrams}

Diagrams are standalone TikZ files in \texttt{.setup/latex/tikz}.
\texttt{make latex} compiles each to PDF, then \texttt{pdftoppm -r 300} makes the PNG;
each is rebuilt only when its source is newer.

\section{Animations}

\begin{itemize}
	\item GIFs are allowed everywhere: pkgdown, Quarto, README, Reveal.js, Beamer, and papers.
	\item \texttt{GifWrite(fig, frames, fps, size)} calls a \texttt{Fig*()} once per frame
	and writes \texttt{.setup/latex/figures/gif/<name>.gif}, the PNG frames,
	and a multi-page PDF, using \texttt{magick}.
	\item Papers show small multiples: a few chosen frames as one faceted PDF figure.
	A paper may also embed the animation with the \texttt{animate} package
	(it plays only in Acrobat and Okular),
	always next to the small multiples, never in place of them.
	\item Under 10 MB per GIF; 2 to 10 frames per second; loops.
	\item No flashing faster than 3 per second.
	Every GIF has alt text and a caption that says what changes.
	Meaning is never shown by colour alone.
\end{itemize}

\section{Tables}

\begin{itemize}
	\item Read results from \texttt{.setup/data-*} and select columns by name, never by number.
	\item \texttt{.Tabular(x = x, digits = 3)} writes the rows;
	\texttt{\textbackslash rinline\{\}} puts them in a hand-written \texttt{booktabs}
	and \texttt{threeparttable} frame.
	\item The web version is \texttt{knitr::kable()} from the same data.
\end{itemize}

\section{Floats}

\begin{itemize}
	\item APA 7: the caption above, as a title in title case;
	\texttt{\textbackslash textit\{Note.\}} below.
	\item Every float and subfigure has a label,
	\texttt{fig:<project>-<name>} or \texttt{tab:<project>-<name>}.
	\item Every figure chunk has a label,
	since an unnamed chunk's file name shifts when a chunk is added.
\end{itemize}

\section{Helpers}

The template writes copies, with no dependency on rProject:
\texttt{.setup/r-miscellaneous/texTools}
(\texttt{.Tabular()}, \texttt{.Round()}, \texttt{.Array()}, the biblatex helpers, \texttt{GifWrite()}, \texttt{GifFrames()})
and \texttt{.setup/r-dependencies} (the \texttt{ggplot2} theme and palette, copied into \texttt{R}).

\section{Tools}

\texttt{make lint} reports (never fails on)
\texttt{scale} and missing files in \texttt{\textbackslash includegraphics},
floats without a caption or label,
figure chunks without a label,
GIFs over 10 MB or without a source,
and papers that embed an animation without small multiples.

